ight over πR² but emits over 4πR². In the simplest equilibrium model, (1 − α)S/4 = εσT⁴.","At equilibrium, absorbed solar power equals outgoing thermal power. Surface and atmospheric transfers are internal to the whole-planet budget, so do not count them twice."]},"ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6:a":{"hint":"Draw an energy boundary around the whole planet. Which incoming and outgoing arrows cross that boundary?","notes":["A spherical planet intercepts sunlight over πR² but emits over 4πR². In the simplest equilibrium model, (1 − α)S/4 = εσT⁴.","At equilibrium, absorbed solar power equals outgoing thermal power. Surface and atmospheric transfers are internal to the whole-planet budget, so do not count them twice."]},"duplicate-814a768cde819a23":{"hint":"Compare the wavelengths of incoming sunlight and radiation emitted by the cooler surface.","notes":["Greenhouse gases absorb and emit selected infrared wavelengths associated with molecular energy changes. Much incoming shortwave sunlight passes through the atmosphere.","After an increase in infrared absorption, the planet warms until outgoing energy again matches absorbed sunlight. Avoid describing the greenhouse effect as reflection of all heat or destruction of energy."]},"duplicate-c41696ff092d4982":{"hint":"Draw an energy boundary around the whole planet. Which incoming and outgoing arrows cross that boundary?","notes":["A spherical planet intercepts sunlight over πR² but emits over 4πR². In the simplest equilibrium model, (1 − α)S/4 = εσT⁴.","At equilibrium, absorbed solar power equals outgoing thermal power. Surface and atmospheric transfers are internal to the whole-planet budget, so do not count them twice.","For “show that”, start from the supplied data and display the intermediate steps. Do not substitute the result you are asked to show as an assumption."]},"duplicate-0d47507e50cd4561":{"hint":"Check the emitting area and use absolute temperature. The temperature dependence is much stronger than linear.","notes":["Thermal emission power is P = εσAT⁴. For a sphere use 4πR²; for a black body ε = 1.","For ratios, keep any changes in emissivity and area as well as the fourth power of temperature. Net exchange with surroundings at Tₛ is εσA(T⁴ − Tₛ⁴) in the stated simple model.","Use any result supplied above directly where needed. You do not need to redo an earlier calculation from another topic."]},"duplicate-8652d9363cbf9df2":{"hint":"Check the emitting area and use absolute temperature. The temperature dependence is much stronger than linear.","notes":["Thermal emission power is P = εσAT⁴. For a sphere use 4πR²; for a black body ε = 1.","For ratios, keep any changes in emissivity and area as well as the fourth power of temperature. Net exchange with surroundings at Tₛ is εσA(T⁴ − Tₛ⁴) in the stated simple model."]},"duplicate-feb0fc08383de6e8":{"hint":"Compare the total mass or binding energy of the initial particles with that of the final particles.","notes":["Energy released is (mass_initial − mass_final)c². Equivalently, it is the increase in total binding energy, not simply the difference in binding energy per nucleon.","When given binding energy per nucleon, multiply by the nucleon number first. Keep atomic and nuclear masses consistent so electron masses cancel correctly."]},"duplicate-db5a4659ecf7f128":{"hint":"Consider both the repulsion between nuclei and the chance of collisions while the fuel is confined.","notes":["Fusion combines light nuclei into more tightly bound products. High temperature helps nuclei approach, high density increases collision rates, and confinement time allows reactions to occur.","Energy release comes from the mass difference or increase in total binding energy. High temperature is a condition for fusion; it is not the source of the released nuclear energy."]},"duplicate-fd6e0c9a76b58ae3":{"hint":"Find the difference between the two relevant energy levels, not the energy of just one level.","notes":["For an atomic transition, photon energy is |E_final − E_initial| = hf = hc/λ. Downward transitions emit; upward transitions require absorption.","Use the positive magnitude of the gap, converting eV to J if using h in SI units. A larger gap corresponds to a shorter wavelength."]},"duplicate-27a3d90b61333688":{"hint":"Check which way surface temperature increases on the horizontal axis before locating the star.","notes":["On a standard H–R diagram luminosity increases upwards and temperature increases to the left. Red giants are luminous but cool; white dwarfs are hot but faint.","Use L = 4πR²σT⁴ to compare radii. A hotter star is not necessarily more luminous, because emitting area also matters."]},"duplicate-ae50fe7451e4501e":{"hint":"Consider both the repulsion between nuclei and the chance of collisions while the fuel is confined.","notes":["Fusion combines light nuclei into more tightly bound products. High temperature helps nuclei approach, high density increases collision rates, and confinement time allows reactions to occur.","Energy release comes from the mass difference or increase in total binding energy. High temperature is a condition for fusion; it is not the source of the released nuclear energy."]},"ibd-4b65378e50ff4d81-q9:whole":{"hint":"Separate the reflected part of the incoming sunlight from the part that is absorbed.","notes":["Albedo is reflected solar power divided by incident solar power. For an opaque surface, absorbed fraction is 1 − albedo.","Use the same system boundary for numerator and denominator. For a planet, add reflected contributions that leave the planet; do not count outgoing infrared as reflected sunlight."]},"duplicate-ce78ab0ac3739484":{"hint":"Compare the wavelengths of incoming sunlight and radiation emitted by the cooler surface.","notes":["Greenhouse gases absorb and emit selected infrared wavelengths associated with molecular energy changes. Much incoming shortwave sunlight passes through the atmosphere.","After an increase in infrared absorption, the planet warms until outgoing energy again matches absorbed sunlight. Avoid describing the greenhouse effect as reflection of all heat or destruction of energy."]},"duplicate-1d882d63aed3d76f":{"hint":"Draw an energy boundary around the whole planet. Which incoming and outgoing arrows cross that boundary?","notes":["A spherical planet intercepts sunlight over πR² but emits over 4πR². In the simplest equilibrium model, (1 − α)S/4 = εσT⁴.","At equilibrium, absorbed solar power equals outgoing thermal power. Surface and atmospheric transfers are internal to the whole-planet budget, so do not count them twice."]},"duplicate-5bca9b4e00754fa5":{"hint":"Draw an energy boundary around the whole planet. Which incoming and outgoing arrows cross that boundary?","notes":["A spherical planet intercepts sunlight over πR² but emits over 4πR². In the simplest equilibrium model, (1 − α)S/4 = εσT⁴.","At equilibrium, absorbed solar power equals outgoing thermal power. Surface and atmospheric transfers are internal to the whole-planet budget, so do not count them twice."]},"ibd-0aba2216d4c0af13-q27:whole":{"hint":"Distinguish changes in the Sun itself from changes in how its radiation reaches a particular place.","notes":["Local sunlight varies with incidence angle, day length, latitude and cloud cover. A more oblique beam spreads its energy over a larger surface area.","The ordinary daily cycle is mainly caused by Earth’s rotation; it does not require the Sun’s power output to rise and fall each day."]},"ibd-c0351bd864435118-q30:whole":{"hint":"Compare the wavelengths of incoming sunlight and radiation emitted by the cooler surface.","notes":["Greenhouse gases absorb and emit selected infrared wavelengths associated with molecular energy changes. Much incoming shortwave sunlight passes through the atmosphere.","After an increase in infrared absorption, the planet warms until outgoing energy again matches absorbed sunlight. Avoid describing the greenhouse effect as reflection of all heat or destruction of energy."]}}},"answers":{"entries":{}},"presentation":{"practice/supplement/sup-eecbb1136ed5ae6d-q9-96d470f7cff709f1.webp":{"crop":[0,1,1124,782],"size":[1124,882],"masks":[[129,6,14,25]]},"practice/supplement/sup-eecbb1136ed5ae6d-q9-8ec8cc1598d854d3.webp":{"hidden":true},"practice/parts/ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6-a-d0e1d273869e845a.webp":{"crop":[0,5,1051,182],"size":[1051,204]},"practice/parts/ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6-b.ii-c92af6b6ce2cbfec.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1051,290],"size":[1051,311]},"practice/parts/ibd-c14ce637c3e6085c-q6-b.ii-e3a48332096fb8fd.webp":{"crop":[0,0,1545,358],"size":[1545,412]},"practice/parts/ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6-b.iii-543dcf33ca7ab41e.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1051,371],"size":[1051,392]},"practice/parts/ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6-c-1b5c11727aedcf5c.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1051,290],"size":[1051,305]},"practice/parts/ibd-c14ce637c3e6085c-q6-c-4cba9dc20a1ffe36.webp":{"crop":[0,0,1545,264],"size":[1545,713]},"practice/parts/ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6-d.i-61d27fa6e82e79f9.webp":{"crop":[0,5,1051,290],"size":[1051,311]},"practice/parts/ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6-d.ii-df64ec03be70aa98.webp":{"crop":[0,5,1051,264],"size":[1051,285]},"practice/parts/ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6-d.iii-93cbe35631f5c11d.webp":{"crop":[0,5,1051,263],"size":[1051,279]},"practice/parts/ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6-e.i-919184d59de774b1.webp":{"crop":[0,5,1051,182],"size":[1051,203]},"practice/parts/ibd-c14ce637c3e6085c-q6-e.i-97c501d1e04e806f.webp":{"crop":[0,0,1545,58],"size":[1545,115]},"practice/parts/ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6-e.ii-de8d71d558e3b6be.webp":{"crop":[0,5,1051,477],"size":[1051,639]},"practice/parts/ibd-c14ce637c3e6085c-q6-e.ii-0782ff1c47ac4857.webp":{"crop":[0,0,1545,535],"size":[1545,902]},"practice/parts/ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6-context-after-a-2d1b7d1b3971aca3.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1051,655],"size":[1051,677]},"practice/parts/ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6-context-d-6667af89a3ce8f67.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1051,146],"size":[1051,172]},"practice/parts/ibd-2ec61b991e61a649-q6-context-e-f66c99518017e